WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.340
Quick reminder that we have more detailed maps&nbsp;
of the surface of Mars than the Earth's ocean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:10.380
floor. In fact, there's a map for that. This is&nbsp;
a map that highlights in dark blue what part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:15.960
the ocean floor is still unmapped. It also shows&nbsp;
the percentage of unmapped territory on the shores&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:19.860
of different countries. Japan's doing pretty&nbsp;
good. They've mapped most of their ocean floor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:24.240
while Thailand has barely mapped any of their&nbsp;
ocean. And the U.S. still has about a third of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:29.280
its oceans unmapped. The ocean makes up&nbsp;
like 70% of the Earth's surface. So why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.780
don't we have a good map of the majority&nbsp;
of our planet? It comes down to a lack of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:38.280
will. It's not like it's crazy expensive. It&nbsp;
would cost an estimated $3 billion to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:42.240
a detailed map of the entire ocean floor.&nbsp;
That's like a rounding error for, like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:47.640
the US military or Jeff Bezos. NASA's spent around&nbsp;
that much, $3 billion, to send the Perseverance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:52.260
Rover to Mars, and billions more on previous Mars&nbsp;
missions. Which, I get it. That's really useful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:56.520
fascinating stuff. But wouldn't it be equally&nbsp;
so to discover what our own planet looks like?

